MAKE(1)                   UC.OS Manual                   MAKE(1)

NAME
       make - keep files up to date by running recipes

SYNOPSIS
       make [options] [variable=value...] [target...]

DESCRIPTION
       make reads a makefile (GNUmakefile, makefile or Makefile, or
       the one named with -f) and remakes each target whose file is
       missing or older than one of its prerequisites. It reads the
       makefiles people write for GNU Make 4.4: the same syntax, the
       same functions and the same messages.

              $ cat Makefile
              hello: hello.o
              $ make
              cc    -c -o hello.o hello.c
              cc   hello.o   -o hello

OPTIONS
       -B         remake every target
       -C dir     change to dir first
       -e         environment variables win over the makefile
       -f file    read file as the makefile (- is standard input)
       -i         ignore errors from recipes
       -I dir     look for included makefiles in dir
       -j [N]     run up to N recipes at once (no N: no limit)
       -k         go on with other targets after an error
       -n         print the recipes without running them
       -o file    treat file as very old; never remake it
       -p         print the rules and variables
       -q         run nothing; exit status 1 if something is out
                  of date
       -r, -R     no built-in rules, no built-in variables
       -s         do not echo recipes
       -t         touch targets instead of running their recipes
       -w         print the working directory
       -W file    treat file as just modified
       --trace    say why each target is remade
       --eval=text, --no-print-directory, --warn-undefined-variables
                  as in GNU Make
       -E -h -l -L -O -R -S -v
                  also accepted, with GNU Make's meaning; -d, -b and -m
                  are accepted and print or do nothing extra

WHAT IS THERE
       Rules with several targets, pattern rules, static pattern
       rules, double-colon rules, order-only prerequisites, grouped
       targets (&:), old-style suffix rules, the built-in rules for
       C, C++, assembler, lex and yacc (CC is cc), VPATH and vpath,
       archive members lib(member), .SECONDEXPANSION and the other
       special targets. Variables of both flavours (= := ::= :::=
       ?= += !=), override, export, define, target-specific and
       pattern-specific variables, automatic variables. Every GNU
       Make function except guile. include and -include, with
       remaking of included makefiles. Conditionals. Recursive make
       with $(MAKE) and MAKEFLAGS.

NOTES
       Recipes run through /bin/sh. A line with no shell syntax is
       started directly. UC.OS keeps file times in whole seconds: a
       prerequisite changed in the same second as its target does
       not count as newer.

       There is no jobserver: a sub-make given -jN runs its own N
       jobs. With one CPU thread, -j gives no speed-up.

       Ctrl-C stops make and its recipe together; a target the recipe
       was writing is not deleted.

EXIT STATUS
       0 when everything is up to date or was remade, 1 with -q when
       something is out of date, 2 on an error.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1)
